/* Past-year questions for Rank mode · JEE Mains · Units and Measurements, grouped by topic and sub-topic, newest first.
   Sources: JEE Main (2019-2025), JEE Advanced and IIT-JEE (1983-2025), AIEEE (2002-2013). Wording adapted; options as in the paper.
   MCQ: o + c (index or array of indices). Numerical: n. Match/column type: a. s = worked solution. y = exam and year. */
window.PhysicaRankPyq=[
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The unit of √{2I;ε₀c} is (I = intensity of an electromagnetic wave, c = speed of light)","o":["NC⁻¹","NC","Nm","Vm"],"c":0,"s":"Intensity I = (energy density) × c, and the energy density is ½ε₀E². So 2I/(ε₀c) = E², and the square root is the electric field E, whose unit is N C⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2025","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"A temperature difference can generate e.m.f. in some materials. Let S be the e.m.f. produced per unit temperature difference between the ends of a wire, σ the electrical conductivity and κ the thermal conductivity of the material of the wire. Taking M, L, T, I and K as dimensions of mass, length, time, current and temperature respectively, the dimensional formula of the quantity Z = {S²σ;κ} is","o":["[M⁰L⁰T⁰I⁰K⁰]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰I⁰K⁻¹]","[M¹L²T⁻²I⁻¹K⁻¹]","[M¹L²T⁻⁴I⁻¹K⁻¹]"],"c":1,"s":"S = V/K = ML²T⁻³I⁻¹K⁻¹, σ = M⁻¹L⁻³T³I²K⁰, κ = MLT⁻³K⁻¹. S²σ = MLT⁻³K⁻². Dividing by κ gives K⁻¹, so Z = [M⁰L⁰T⁰I⁰K⁻¹]."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"In a measurement, it is asked to find the modulus of elasticity per unit torque applied on the system. The measured quantity has dimension of [MᵃLᵇTᶜ]. If b = 3, the value of c is ______.","n":0,"s":"Modulus of elasticity is ML⁻¹T⁻² and torque is ML²T⁻². Their ratio has no mass and no time in it, only a power of length (L³ in the form the question uses). So c = 0."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"If ε₀ is the permittivity of free space and E is the electric field, then ε₀E² has the dimensions","o":["[M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻²]","[M⁰L⁻²TA]"],"c":1,"s":"ε₀E² is the electric energy density: energy/volume = ML²T⁻²/L³ = ML⁻¹T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimensional formula of latent heat is","o":["[M⁰L²T⁻²]","[M⁰LT⁻²]","[ML²T⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]"],"c":0,"s":"Latent heat is heat per unit mass: ML²T⁻²/M = M⁰L²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 9 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The de-Broglie wavelength associated with a particle of mass m and energy E is {h;√(2mE)}. The dimensional formula for Planck’s constant is","o":["[MLT⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻¹]","[M²L²T⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"h = λ√(2mE). [√(mE)] = √(M · ML²T⁻²) = MLT⁻¹. So [h] = L · MLT⁻¹ = ML²T⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimensional formula of angular impulse is","o":["[ML²T⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻²]","[MLT⁻¹]","[ML⁻²T⁻¹]"],"c":0,"s":"Angular impulse = torque × time = ML²T⁻² × T = ML²T⁻¹ (the same as angular momentum)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimensions of ({B²;μ₀}) will be (μ₀ = permeability of free space, B = magnetic field)","o":["[ML²T⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻²A⁻¹]"],"c":2,"s":"B²/2μ₀ is the magnetic energy density, which is energy/volume = ML⁻¹T⁻². B²/μ₀ has the same dimensions."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimension of mutual inductance is","o":["[ML²T⁻²A⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻³A⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻²A⁻²]","[ML²T⁻³A⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"Mutual inductance M = Φ/I, where flux Φ = ML²T⁻²A⁻¹. So M = ML²T⁻²A⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The SI unit of a physical quantity is pascal-second. The dimensional formula of this quantity will be","o":["[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]","[ML⁻¹T⁻²]","[ML²T⁻¹]","[M⁻¹L³T⁰]"],"c":0,"s":"Pascal = ML⁻¹T⁻². Multiplying by a second gives ML⁻¹T⁻¹ (coefficient of viscosity)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"If E and H represent the intensity of electric field and the magnetising field respectively, then the unit of E/H will be","o":["joule","ohm","newton","mho"],"c":1,"s":"E is in V/m and H is in A/m, so E/H is in V/A, which is the ohm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"If E, L, M and G denote the quantities as energy, angular momentum, mass and the constant of gravitation respectively, then the dimensions of P in the formula P = EL²M⁻⁵G⁻² are","o":["[M¹L¹T⁻²]","[M⁰L¹T⁰]","[M⁻¹L⁻¹T²]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰]"],"c":3,"s":"E = ML²T⁻², L² = M²L⁴T⁻², M⁻⁵, G⁻² = M²L⁻⁶T⁴. Mass: 1 + 2 − 5 + 2 = 0. Length: 2 + 4 − 6 = 0. Time: −2 − 2 + 4 = 0. So P has no dimensions."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"If C and V represent capacity and voltage respectively, then what are the dimensions of λ where C/V = λ?","o":["[M⁻²L⁻⁴I³T⁷]","[M⁻¹L⁻³I⁻²T⁻⁷]","[M⁻²L⁻³I²T⁶]","[M⁻³L⁻⁴I³T⁷]"],"c":0,"s":"[C] = M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴I² and [V] = ML²T⁻³I⁻¹. C/V = M⁻²L⁻⁴T⁷I³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 3 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The amount of solar energy received on the Earth’s surface per unit area per unit time is defined as the solar constant. The dimension of the solar constant is","o":["ML²T⁻²","ML⁰T⁻³","M²L⁰T⁻¹","MLT⁻²"],"c":1,"s":"Energy per area per time = ML²T⁻² / (L² · T) = MT⁻³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimensional formula for thermal conductivity is (here K denotes the temperature)","o":["MLT⁻²K⁻²","MLT⁻³K⁻¹","MLT⁻³K","MLT⁻²K"],"c":1,"s":"Heat flow per time = κ A ΔT/Δx. So κ = (ML²T⁻³) L/(L² K) = MLT⁻³K⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 7 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimension of {B²;2μ₀}, where B is the magnetic field and μ₀ is the magnetic permeability of vacuum, is","o":["MLT⁻²","ML²T⁻¹","ML²T⁻²","ML⁻¹T⁻²"],"c":3,"s":"B²/2μ₀ is the magnetic energy density, energy/volume = ML⁻¹T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"In SI units, the dimensions of √{ε₀;μ₀} is","o":["A²T³M⁻¹L⁻²","AT²M⁻¹L⁻¹","AT⁻³ML^(3/2)","A⁻¹TML³"],"c":0,"s":"√(μ₀/ε₀) has the dimensions of resistance, ML²T⁻³A⁻². Its inverse √(ε₀/μ₀) is a conductance: M⁻¹L⁻²T³A²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"Let l, r, c and v represent inductance, resistance, capacitance and voltage respectively. The dimension of {l;rcv} in SI units will be","o":["[LA⁻²]","[LT²]","[A⁻¹]","[LTA]"],"c":2,"s":"l/r is a time T. Then (l/r)/(cv) = T/Q, where Q = cv is charge = AT. So the result is T/(AT) = A⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"In the following, I refers to current and other symbols have their usual meaning. Choose the option that corresponds to the dimensions of electrical conductivity.","o":["[M⁻¹L⁻³T³I]","[M⁻¹L⁻³T³I²]","[M⁻¹L³T³I]","[ML⁻³T⁻³I²]"],"c":1,"s":"Conductivity is the inverse of resistivity. Resistivity = R·A/l = ML²T⁻³I⁻² · L = ML³T⁻³I⁻². Its inverse is M⁻¹L⁻³T³I²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2013","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"Let [ε₀] denote the dimensional formula of the permittivity of vacuum. If M = mass, L = length, T = time and A = electric current, then","o":["[ε₀] = [M⁻¹L²T⁻¹A]","[ε₀] = [M⁻¹L⁻³T²A]","[ε₀] = [M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²]","[ε₀] = [M⁻¹L²T⁻¹A⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"From F = q²/(4πε₀r²), ε₀ = q²/(Fr²) = A²T²/(MLT⁻² · L²) = M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2008","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimension of the magnetic field in M, L, T and C (coulomb) is given as","o":["MT⁻²C⁻¹","MLT⁻¹C⁻¹","MT²C⁻²","MT⁻¹C⁻¹"],"c":3,"s":"B = F/(qv) = MLT⁻² / (C · LT⁻¹) = MT⁻¹C⁻¹."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2006","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"Which of the following units denotes the dimensions ML²/Q², where Q denotes the electric charge?","o":["weber (Wb)","Wb/m²","henry (H)","H/m²"],"c":2,"s":"The henry is ML²T⁻²A⁻². Writing A = Q/T gives ML²T⁻²(T²/Q²) = ML²Q⁻²."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2004","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"Which one of the following represents the correct dimensions of the coefficient of viscosity?","o":["ML⁻¹T⁻²","MLT⁻¹","ML⁻¹T⁻¹","ML⁻²T⁻²"],"c":2,"s":"F = 6πηrv gives η = F/(rv) = MLT⁻²/(L · LT⁻¹) = ML⁻¹T⁻¹."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2000","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The dimensions of ({1;2})ε₀E² is (ε₀: permittivity of free space, E: electric field)","o":["MLT⁻¹","ML²T⁻²","ML⁻¹T⁻²","ML²T⁻¹"],"c":2,"s":"½ε₀E² is the electric energy density = energy/volume = ML²T⁻²/L³ = ML⁻¹T⁻²."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1998","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"The SI unit of inductance, the henry, can be written as","o":["weber/ampere","volt-second/ampere","joule/(ampere)²","ohm-second"],"c":[0,1,2,3],"s":"L = Φ/I gives weber/ampere. emf = L(dI/dt) gives L = V·s/A. Energy = ½LI² gives joule/A². L = (V/I)·s gives ohm-second. All four are correct."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1998","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensional formula and unit of a quantity","q":"Let [ε₀] denote the permittivity of the vacuum and [μ₀] denote the permeability of the vacuum. If M = mass, L = length, T = time and I = electric current, then the dimensional formulas of ε₀ and μ₀ are","o":["[ε₀] = M⁻¹L⁻³T²I","[ε₀] = M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴I²","[μ₀] = MLT⁻²I⁻²","[μ₀] = ML²T⁻¹I"],"c":[1,2],"s":"ε₀ = M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴I² (from F = q²/4πε₀r²) and μ₀ = MLT⁻²I⁻² (from F/l = μ₀I₁I₂/2πr)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Given a charge q, current I and the permeability of vacuum μ₀, which of the following quantities has the dimension of momentum?","o":["qI/μ₀","qμ₀I","qμ₀/I","q²μ₀I"],"c":1,"s":"[q] = AT and [μ₀] = MLT⁻²A⁻². qμ₀I = (AT)(MLT⁻²A⁻²)(A) = MLT⁻¹, the dimensions of momentum."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The dimension of √{μ₀;ε₀} is equal to that of (μ₀ = vacuum permeability, ε₀ = vacuum permittivity)","o":["Resistance","Inductance","Capacitance","Voltage"],"c":0,"s":"√(μ₀/ε₀) = E/H, the ratio of electric field to magnetising field, which is V/A, the unit of resistance (about 377 Ω in vacuum)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The pair of physical quantities not having the same dimensions is","o":["pressure and Young’s modulus","surface tension and impulse","angular momentum and Planck’s constant","torque and energy"],"c":1,"s":"Surface tension is force/length = MT⁻². Impulse is force × time = MLT⁻¹. They differ. The other pairs have the same dimensions: ML⁻¹T⁻², ML²T⁻¹ and ML²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"If G is the gravitational constant and u is the energy density, then which of the following quantities has the dimensions of √(uG)?","o":["Gravitational potential","Pressure gradient per unit mass","Energy per unit mass","Force per unit mass"],"c":3,"s":"[u] = ML⁻¹T⁻² and [G] = M⁻¹L³T⁻², so uG = L²T⁻⁴ and √(uG) = LT⁻². That is acceleration, the same as force per unit mass."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The dimension of {1;μ₀ε₀} should be equal to","o":["T/L","L²/T²","T²/L²","L/T"],"c":1,"s":"1/√(μ₀ε₀) = c, the speed of light. So 1/(μ₀ε₀) = c², with dimensions L²/T²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Which of the following physical quantities have the same dimensions?","o":["Electric displacement (D) and surface charge density","Displacement current and electric field","Current density and surface charge density","Electric potential and energy"],"c":0,"s":"D = ε₀E has units of C/m², the same as surface charge density. The other pairs differ (current ≠ field, A/m² ≠ C/m², volt ≠ joule)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Identify the pair of physical quantities which have different dimensions.","o":["Wave number and Rydberg’s constant","Stress and coefficient of elasticity","Coercivity and magnetisation","Specific heat capacity and latent heat"],"c":3,"s":"Specific heat capacity is L²T⁻²K⁻¹ and latent heat is L²T⁻², so they differ. Wave number and Rydberg constant are both L⁻¹, stress and modulus are both ML⁻¹T⁻², coercivity and magnetisation are both A/m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 24 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Identify the pair of physical quantities that have the same dimensions.","o":["velocity gradient and decay constant","Wien’s constant and Stefan constant","angular frequency and angular momentum","wave number and Avogadro number"],"c":0,"s":"Velocity gradient = (LT⁻¹)/L = T⁻¹ and the decay constant is T⁻¹. In the other pairs the dimensions differ."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"If L, C and R are the self inductance, capacitance and resistance respectively, which of the following does not have the dimension of time?","o":["RC","L/R","√(LC)","L/C"],"c":3,"s":"RC, L/R and √(LC) are all time (the time constants and 1/ω of LC and RLC circuits). L/C has the dimensions of resistance squared, not time."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2021","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"A physical quantity S is defined as S = {E × B;μ₀}, where E is the electric field, B is the magnetic field and μ₀ is the permeability of free space. The dimensions of S are the same as the dimensions of which of the following quantity(ies)?","o":["Energy / (Charge × Current)","Force / (Length × Time)","Energy / Volume","Power / Area"],"c":[1,3],"s":"S is the Poynting vector (power per area) = MT⁻³. Force/(length × time) = MLT⁻²/(LT) = MT⁻³ and power/area = ML²T⁻³/L² = MT⁻³. Energy/(charge × current) is ML²T⁻³A⁻² and energy/volume is ML⁻¹T⁻², which differ."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 4 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"A quantity x is given by (IFv²/WL⁴) in terms of moment of inertia I, force F, velocity v, work W and length L. The dimensional formula for x is the same as that of","o":["energy density","coefficient of viscosity","force constant","Planck’s constant"],"c":0,"s":"IFv² = ML² · MLT⁻² · L²T⁻² = M²L⁵T⁻⁴. WL⁴ = ML⁶T⁻². Dividing gives ML⁻¹T⁻², which is the energy density."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The quantities x = {1;√(μ₀ε₀)}, y = {E;B} and z = {l;CR} are defined where C is capacitance, R is resistance, l is length, E is the electric field, B is the magnetic field and ε₀, μ₀ are the free space permittivity and permeability respectively. Then","o":["x, y and z have the same dimension","only x and z have the same dimension","only x and y have the same dimension","only y and z have the same dimension"],"c":0,"s":"x = 1/√(μ₀ε₀) = c, a velocity. y = E/B is a velocity. CR is a time, so z = l/CR is a velocity. All three have the dimensions LT⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 9 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"A quantity f is given by f = √{hc⁵;G}, where c is the speed of light, G is the universal gravitational constant and h is Planck’s constant. The dimension of f is that of","o":["area","volume","momentum","energy"],"c":3,"s":"hc⁵/G = ML²T⁻¹ · L⁵T⁻⁵ / (M⁻¹L³T⁻²) = M²L⁴T⁻⁴. Its square root is ML²T⁻², the dimensions of energy."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Which of the following combinations has the dimension of electrical resistance (ε₀ is the permittivity of vacuum and μ₀ is the permeability of vacuum)?","o":["√{μ₀;ε₀}","√{ε₀;μ₀}","{ε₀;μ₀}","{μ₀;ε₀}"],"c":0,"s":"√(μ₀/ε₀) = E/H = (V/m)/(A/m) = V/A, the unit of resistance (about 377 Ω in vacuum)."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2018","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"In electromagnetic theory, the electric and magnetic phenomena are related to each other, so the dimensions of electric and magnetic quantities are also related. [E] and [B] stand for dimensions of electric and magnetic fields, [ε₀] and [μ₀] for the permittivity and permeability of free space, and [L] and [T] for length and time. The relation between [E] and [B] is","o":["[E] = [B][L][T]","[E] = [B][L]⁻¹[T]","[E] = [B][L][T]⁻¹","[E] = [B][L]⁻¹[T]⁻¹"],"c":2,"s":"E/B has the dimensions of velocity (it is the speed of light in a wave), so [E] = [B][L][T]⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2018","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"In electromagnetic theory, [ε₀] and [μ₀] stand for the dimensions of the permittivity and permeability of free space, and [L] and [T] for length and time. The relation between [ε₀] and [μ₀] is","o":["[μ₀] = [ε₀][L]²[T]⁻²","[μ₀] = [ε₀][L]⁻²[T]²","[μ₀] = [ε₀]⁻¹[L]²[T]⁻²","[μ₀] = [ε₀]⁻¹[L]⁻²[T]²"],"c":3,"s":"c² = 1/(μ₀ε₀) with [c] = LT⁻¹, so [μ₀][ε₀] = L⁻²T². Hence [μ₀] = [ε₀]⁻¹[L]⁻²[T]²."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2005","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Out of the following pairs, which one does not have identical dimensions?","o":["Moment of inertia and moment of a force","Work and torque","Angular momentum and Planck’s constant","Impulse and momentum"],"c":0,"s":"Moment of inertia is ML². The moment of a force (torque) is ML²T⁻². They differ. Work and torque, angular momentum and h, and impulse and momentum each match."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2005","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Which of the following sets have different dimensions?","o":["Pressure, Young’s modulus, stress","Emf, potential difference, electric potential","Heat, work done, energy","Dipole moment, electric flux, electric field"],"c":3,"s":"Pressure, modulus and stress are all ML⁻¹T⁻². Emf, potential difference and potential are all ML²T⁻³A⁻¹. Heat, work and energy are all ML²T⁻². Dipole moment (LTA), flux (ML³T⁻³A⁻¹) and field (MLT⁻³A⁻¹) differ."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2003","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The physical quantities not having the same dimensions are","o":["torque and work","momentum and Planck’s constant","stress and Young’s modulus","speed and (μ₀ε₀)^(−1/2)"],"c":1,"s":"Momentum is MLT⁻¹ and Planck’s constant is ML²T⁻¹, so they differ. The other pairs match: torque and work (ML²T⁻²), stress and modulus (ML⁻¹T⁻²), speed and 1/√(μ₀ε₀) (LT⁻¹)."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2002","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Identify the pair whose dimensions are equal.","o":["Torque and work","Stress and energy","Force and stress","Force and work"],"c":0,"s":"Torque and work are both ML²T⁻². Stress is ML⁻¹T⁻² and energy is ML²T⁻², so they differ. Force is MLT⁻²."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2001","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"A quantity X is given by ε₀L{ΔV;Δt}, where ε₀ is the permittivity of free space, L is a length, ΔV is a potential difference and Δt is a time interval. The dimensional formula for X is the same as that of","o":["resistance","charge","voltage","current"],"c":3,"s":"ε₀L is a capacitance (charge per volt). So ε₀LΔV is a charge, and charge per time is a current."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1995","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The pairs of physical quantities that have the same dimensions in (are)","o":["Reynolds number and coefficient of friction","Curie and frequency of a light wave","Latent heat and gravitational potential","Planck’s constant and torque"],"c":[0,1,2],"s":"Reynolds number and the coefficient of friction have no dimensions. The curie (decays per second) and frequency are T⁻¹. Latent heat and gravitational potential are L²T⁻². Planck’s constant ML²T⁻¹ differs from torque ML²T⁻²."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1986","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"The dimensions of the quantities in one (or more) of the following pairs are the same. Identify the pair(s).","o":["Torque and work","Angular momentum and work","Energy and Young’s modulus","Light year and wavelength"],"c":[0,3],"s":"Torque and work are both ML²T⁻². A light year and a wavelength are both lengths. Angular momentum (ML²T⁻¹) and work differ, and energy (ML²T⁻²) and Young’s modulus (ML⁻¹T⁻²) differ."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1986","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"Which of the following combinations have the dimensions of time? L, C, R represent inductance, capacitance and resistance respectively.","o":["RC","√(LC)","R/L","C/L"],"c":[0,1],"s":"RC and √(LC) are times. R/L is a frequency (1/time), and C/L has the dimensions of 1/resistance²."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1984","tp":"dims","st":"Same dimensions and equivalent quantities","q":"If L, C, R represent inductance, capacitance and resistance respectively, the combinations having dimensions of frequency are","o":["{1;√(CL)}","{L;C}","{R;L}","{R;C}"],"c":[0,2],"s":"√(LC) is a time, so 1/√(LC) is a frequency. R/L is 1/time, also a frequency. L/C has the dimensions of resistance squared, and R/C is not a frequency."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensionless quantities","q":"Which of the following is not a dimensionless quantity?","o":["Power factor","Quality factor","Permeability of free space (μ₀)","Relative magnetic permeability (μᵣ)"],"c":2,"s":"Power factor, quality factor and relative permeability are ratios and have no dimensions. μ₀ = MLT⁻²A⁻² does have dimensions."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Dimensionless quantities","q":"If e is the electronic charge, c is the speed of light in free space and h is Planck’s constant, the quantity {1;4πε₀}{|e|²;hc} has dimensions of","o":["[M L T⁻¹]","[L C⁻¹]","[M L T⁰]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰]"],"c":3,"s":"e²/(4πε₀) is energy × length = ML³T⁻², and hc = ML²T⁻¹ · LT⁻¹ = ML³T⁻². The ratio has no dimensions (it is the fine-structure constant)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The equation for a real gas is (P + {a;V²})(V − b) = RT, where P, V, T and R are the pressure, volume, temperature and gas constant respectively. The dimension of ab⁻² is equivalent to that of","o":["Compressibility","Strain","Planck’s constant","Energy density"],"c":3,"s":"[a] = [P][V²] = ML⁵T⁻² and [b] = [V] = L³. ab⁻² = ML⁵T⁻² / L⁶ = ML⁻¹T⁻², the dimensions of pressure, which are the same as energy density."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The position of a particle moving on the x-axis is given by x(t) = A sin t + B cos² t + Ct² + D, where t is time. The dimension of {ABC;D} is","o":["L³T⁻²","L²","L²T⁻²","L"],"c":2,"s":"Every term has the dimension of length, so [A] = [B] = [D] = L and [C] = LT⁻². ABC/D = (L)(L)(LT⁻²)/L = L²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The energy E and momentum p of a moving body of mass m are related by some equation. Given that c represents the speed of light, identify the correct equation.","o":["E² = pc² + m²c⁴","E² = pc² + m²c²","E² = p²c² + m²c⁴","E² = p²c² + m²c²"],"c":2,"s":"E² has dimensions M²L⁴T⁻⁴. p²c² = M²L²T⁻² · L²T⁻² and m²c⁴ = M² L⁴T⁻⁴ both match. The relativistic relation is E² = p²c² + m²c⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The expression given below shows the variation of velocity v with time t: v = At² + {Bt;C + t}. The dimension of ABC is","o":["[M⁰L²T⁻²]","[M⁰L¹T⁻³]","[M⁰L²T⁻³]","[M⁰L¹T⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"C is added to t, so [C] = T. At² is a velocity, so [A] = LT⁻³. Bt/(C + t) is a velocity and t/(C + t) has no dimensions, so [B] = LT⁻¹. ABC = LT⁻³ · LT⁻¹ · T = L²T⁻³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The equation of a stationary wave is y = 2a sin({2πnt;λ}) cos({2πx;λ}). Which of the following is not correct?","o":["The dimensions of n is [LT⁻¹]","The dimensions of x is [L]","The dimensions of nt is [L]","The dimensions of {n;λ} is [T]"],"c":3,"s":"The angles 2πnt/λ and 2πx/λ have no dimensions. So [nt] = [λ] = L, giving [n] = LT⁻¹, and [x] = L. n/λ = LT⁻¹/L = T⁻¹, not T. So (d) is wrong."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 4 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"Applying the principle of homogeneity of dimensions, determine which one is correct, where T is the time period, G is the gravitational constant, M is the mass and r is the radius of orbit.","o":["T² = {4π²r²;GM}","T² = {4π²r³;GM}","T² = 4π²r³","T² = {4π²r;GM²}"],"c":1,"s":"[GM] = M⁻¹L³T⁻² · M = L³T⁻². So r³/(GM) has dimensions T². Option (b) is the dimensionally correct one (Kepler’s third law)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"What is the dimensional formula of ab⁻¹ in the equation (P + {a;V²})(V − b) = RT, where the letters have their usual meaning?","o":["[ML²T⁻²]","[M⁶L⁷T⁴]","[M⁰L³T⁻²]","[M⁻¹L⁵T³]"],"c":0,"s":"[a/V²] = [P], so [a] = ML⁻¹T⁻² · L⁶ = ML⁵T⁻². [b] = [V] = L³. ab⁻¹ = ML⁵T⁻²/L³ = ML²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The equation of state of a real gas is given by (P + {a;V²})(V − b) = RT, where P, V and T are pressure, volume and temperature respectively and R is the universal gas constant. The dimensions of {a;b²} is similar to that of","o":["R","RT","PV","P"],"c":3,"s":"[a] = ML⁵T⁻² and [b²] = L⁶, so a/b² = ML⁻¹T⁻², the dimensions of pressure P."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"A force is represented by F = ax² + bt^(1/2), where x = distance and t = time. The dimensions of b²/a are","o":["[ML²T⁻³]","[ML⁻¹T⁻¹]","[MLT⁻²]","[ML³T⁻³]"],"c":3,"s":"[a] = MLT⁻²/L² = ML⁻¹T⁻² and [b] = MLT⁻²/T^(1/2) = MLT^(−5/2). b²/a = M²L²T⁻⁵ / ML⁻¹T⁻² = ML³T⁻³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"Consider two physical quantities A and B related to each other as E = {B − x²;At}, where E, x and t have dimensions of energy, length and time respectively. The dimension of AB is","o":["L⁰M⁻¹T¹","L⁻²M¹T⁰","L²M⁻¹T¹","L⁻²M⁻¹T¹"],"c":2,"s":"B is subtracted from x², so [B] = L². Then [At] = [B]/[E] = L²/ML²T⁻² = M⁻¹T², so [A] = M⁻¹T. AB = L² · M⁻¹T = L²M⁻¹T¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In the equation [X + {a;Y²}][Y − b] = RT, X is pressure, Y is volume, R is the universal gas constant and T is temperature. The physical quantity equivalent to the ratio a/b is","o":["impulse","coefficient of viscosity","energy","pressure gradient"],"c":2,"s":"[a] = [X][Y²] = ML⁵T⁻² and [b] = [Y] = L³. a/b = ML²T⁻², the dimensions of energy."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The equation of a circle is x² + y² = a², where a is the radius. If the equation is modified to change the origin other than (0, 0), find the correct dimensions of A and B in the new equation (x − At)² + (y − {t;B})² = a². The dimensions of t is given as [T⁻¹].","o":["A = [L⁻¹T⁻¹], B = [LT]","A = [L⁻¹T⁻¹], B = [LT⁻¹]","A = [LT], B = [L⁻¹T⁻¹]","A = [L⁻¹T], B = [LT⁻¹]"],"c":2,"s":"At must be a length: [A][T⁻¹] = L, so [A] = LT. t/B must be a length: [B] = T⁻¹/L = L⁻¹T⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"An expression of energy density is given by u = {α;β} sin({αx;kt}), where α and β are constants, x is displacement, k is the Boltzmann constant and t is the temperature. The dimensions of β will be","o":["[ML²T⁻²θ⁻¹]","[M⁰L²T⁻²]","[M⁰L⁰T⁰]","[M⁰L²T⁰]"],"c":3,"s":"The angle αx/kt has no dimensions, so [α] = [kt]/[x] = ML²T⁻²/L = MLT⁻². Also u = α/β, so [β] = [α]/[u] = MLT⁻²/ML⁻¹T⁻² = L²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine is given by η = {αβ;sin θ} logₑ({βx;kT}), where α and β are constants. If T is temperature, k is the Boltzmann constant, θ is angular displacement and x has the dimensions of length, then choose the incorrect option.","o":["Dimensions of β is same as that of force","Dimensions of α⁻¹x is same as that of energy","Dimensions of η⁻¹ sin θ is same as that of αβ","Dimensions of α is same as that of β"],"c":3,"s":"βx/kT has no dimensions, so [β] = kT/x = ML²T⁻²/L = MLT⁻², a force. αβ has no dimensions, so α = 1/β. Then α⁻¹x = βx is force × length, an energy. η⁻¹ sin θ and αβ both have no dimensions. But [α] = 1/[β], not equal to [β], so (d) is incorrect."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"An expression for a dimensionless quantity P is given by P = {α;β} logₑ({kt;βx}), where α and β are constants, x is distance, k is the Boltzmann constant and t is the temperature. Then the dimensions of α will be","o":["[M⁰L⁻¹T⁰]","[ML⁰T⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]","[ML²T⁻²]"],"c":2,"s":"kt/βx has no dimensions, so [β] = kt/x = ML²T⁻²/L = MLT⁻². P is dimensionless and the log has none, so α/β has none: [α] = [β] = MLT⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 26 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"If ρ is the density and η is the coefficient of viscosity of a fluid which flows with a speed v in a pipe of diameter d, the correct formula for the Reynolds number Rₑ is","o":["Rₑ = {ηd;ρv}","Rₑ = {ρv;ηd}","Rₑ = {ρvd;η}","Rₑ = {η;ρvd}"],"c":2,"s":"[ρvd] = ML⁻³ · LT⁻¹ · L = ML⁻¹T⁻¹ = [η]. So ρvd/η has no dimensions, as the Reynolds number must."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In the van der Waals equation [P + {a;V²}][V − b] = RT, P is the pressure, V is the volume, R is the universal gas constant and T is the temperature. The ratio of constants a/b is dimensionally equal to","o":["P/V","V/P","PV","PV³"],"c":2,"s":"[a] = [P][V²] and [b] = [V], so a/b has the dimensions of PV, which is energy."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"Which of the following equations is dimensionally incorrect? (t = time, h = height, s = surface tension, θ = angle, ρ = density, a, r = radius, g = acceleration due to gravity, v = volume, p = pressure, W = work done, τ = torque, ε = permittivity, E = electric field, J = current density, L = length)","o":["W = τθ","v = {πpa⁴;8ηL}","h = {2s cos θ;ρrg}","J = ε {∂E;∂t}"],"c":1,"s":"πpa⁴/(8ηL) is the volume flowing per second (Poiseuille’s law), so it has the dimensions L³T⁻¹, not a volume. The other three are correct: torque × angle is work, capillary rise is dimensionally right, and ε∂E/∂t is the displacement current density."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The entropy of any system is given by S = α²β ln[{μKR;Jβ²} + 3], where α and β are constants. μ, J, K and R are the number of moles, the mechanical equivalent of heat, the Boltzmann constant and the gas constant respectively. [Take S = dQ/T.] Choose the incorrect option.","o":["S, β, k and μR have the same dimensions","α and k have the same dimensions","α and J have the same dimensions","S and α have different dimensions"],"c":1,"s":"The log is dimensionless, so β² has the dimensions of μKR, giving β the dimensions of S (energy per kelvin, the same as k and μR). Then S = α²β shows α has no dimensions, as J has none. So α and k do not have the same dimensions, and (b) is incorrect."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The force is given in terms of time t and displacement x by the equation F = A cos Bx + C sin Dt. The dimensional formula of {AD;B} is","o":["[M²L²T⁻³]","[M¹L¹T⁻²]","[M⁰LT⁻¹]","[ML²T⁻³]"],"c":3,"s":"Bx and Dt have no dimensions, so [B] = L⁻¹ and [D] = T⁻¹. A and C have the dimensions of force, MLT⁻². AD/B = MLT⁻² · T⁻¹ / L⁻¹ = ML²T⁻³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The work done by a gas molecule in an isolated system is given by W = αβ² e^(−x²/αkT), where x is the displacement, k is the Boltzmann constant and T is the temperature. α and β are constants. Then the dimensions of β will be","o":["[MLT⁻²]","[M⁰LT⁰]","[ML²T⁻²]","[M²LT²]"],"c":0,"s":"x²/(αkT) has no dimensions, so [α] = L²/(ML²T⁻²) = M⁻¹T². Then W = αβ² gives ML²T⁻² = M⁻¹T²[β²], so [β²] = M²L²T⁻⁴ and [β] = MLT⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In a typical combustion engine the work done by a gas molecule is given by W = α²β e^(−βx²/kT), where x is the displacement, k is the Boltzmann constant and T is the temperature. If α and β are constants, the dimensions of α will be","o":["[M²LT⁻²]","[MLT⁻²]","[MLT⁻¹]","[M⁰LT⁰]"],"c":3,"s":"βx²/kT has no dimensions, so [β] = kT/x² = ML²T⁻²/L² = MT⁻². Then α² = W/β = ML²T⁻²/MT⁻² = L², so [α] = L."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In the formula X = 5YZ², X and Z have dimensions of capacitance and magnetic field respectively. What are the dimensions of Y in SI units?","o":["[M⁻²L⁻²T⁶A³]","[M⁻³L⁻²T⁸A⁴]","[M⁻²L⁰T⁻⁴A⁻²]","[M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A²]"],"c":1,"s":"Capacitance = M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A² and magnetic field = MT⁻²A⁻¹. Y = X/(5Z²) = M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A² / M²T⁻⁴A⁻² = M⁻³L⁻²T⁸A⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"The force of interaction between two atoms is given by F = αβ exp(−{x²;αkT}), where x is the distance, k is the Boltzmann constant and T is the temperature, and α and β are two constants. The dimension of β is","o":["M²L²T⁻²","M⁰L²T⁻⁴","MLT⁻²","M²LT⁻⁴"],"c":3,"s":"x²/(αkT) has no dimensions, so [α] = L²/(ML²T⁻²) = M⁻¹T². Then [β] = [F]/[α] = MLT⁻² / M⁻¹T² = M²LT⁻⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"A, B, C and D are four different physical quantities having different dimensions. None of them is dimensionless. But we know that the equation AD = C ln(BD) holds true. Then which of the combinations is not a meaningful quantity?","o":["{C;BD} − {AD²;C}","A² − B²C²","{A;B} − C","{A − C;D}"],"c":[0,3],"s":"ln(BD) has no dimensions, so [B] = 1/[D] and [AD] = [C]. (b) B²C² = (C/D)² = A², fine. (c) A/B = AD = C, fine. (a) C/(BD) has the dimensions of C but AD²/C has the dimensions of D, and C ≠ D. (d) A − C would subtract quantities of different dimensions (C = AD). So (a) and (d) are not meaningful."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2015","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In terms of potential difference V, electric current I, permittivity ε₀, permeability μ₀ and speed of light c, the dimensionally correct equation(s) is (are)","o":["μ₀I² = ε₀V²","ε₀I = μ₀V","I = ε₀cV","μ₀cI = ε₀V"],"c":[0,2],"s":"Both μ₀I² and ε₀V² have the dimensions of force (MLT⁻²). ε₀cV has the dimensions of charge/time, a current. In (b) and (d) the two sides differ in dimensions."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2004","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"Pressure depends on distance as P = {α;β} exp({−αz;kθ}), where α and β are constants, z is distance, k is the Boltzmann constant and θ is temperature. The dimensions of β are","o":["M⁰L⁰T⁰","M⁻¹L⁻¹T⁻¹","M⁰L²T⁰","M⁻¹L⁻¹T²"],"c":2,"s":"The exponent has no dimensions, so [α] = kθ/z = MLT⁻². Then [β] = [α]/[P] = MLT⁻² / ML⁻¹T⁻² = L²."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1995","tp":"dims","st":"Homogeneity and constants in an equation","q":"In the formula X = 3YZ², X and Z have dimensions of capacitance and magnetic induction respectively. What are the dimensions of Y in the MKSQ system?","o":["[M⁻³L⁻¹T³Q⁴]","[M⁻³L⁻²T⁴Q⁴]","[M⁻²L⁻²T⁴Q⁴]","[M⁻³L⁻²T⁴Q¹]"],"c":1,"s":"Capacitance = Q²T²M⁻¹L⁻² and magnetic field = MT⁻¹Q⁻¹. Y = X/(3Z²) = Q²T²M⁻¹L⁻² / (M²T⁻²Q⁻²) = M⁻³L⁻²T⁴Q⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"If mass is written as m = k c^P G^(−1/2) h^(1/2), then the value of P will be (constants have their usual meaning, with k a dimensionless constant)","o":["−1/3","1/2","2","1/3"],"c":1,"s":"G^(−1/2)h^(1/2) = M^(1/2)L^(−3/2)T · M^(1/2)L T^(−1/2) = M L^(−1/2) T^(1/2). Mass needs no L and T, so c^P = L^(1/2)T^(−1/2) = (LT⁻¹)^(1/2). P = 1/2."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2024","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"A dimensionless quantity is constructed in terms of the electronic charge e, the permittivity of free space ε₀, Planck’s constant h and the speed of light c. If the dimensionless quantity is written as e^α ε₀^β h^γ c^δ and n is a non-zero integer, then (α, β, γ, δ) is given by","o":["(2n, −n, −n, −n)","(n, −n, −2n, −n)","(n, −n, −n, −2n)","(2n, −n, −2n, −2n)"],"c":0,"s":"The combination e²/(ε₀hc) has no dimensions (it is the fine-structure constant, up to 4π²). Raising it to the power n gives (α, β, γ, δ) = (2n, −n, −n, −n)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 15 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"The speed of a wave produced in water is given by v = λᵃgᵇρᶜ, where λ, g and ρ are the wavelength of the wave, the acceleration due to gravity and the density of water respectively. The values of a, b and c respectively are","o":["1/2, 0, 1/2","1, −1, 0","1, 1, 0","1/2, 1/2, 0"],"c":3,"s":"Mass appears only in ρ, so c = 0. Then LT⁻¹ = Lᵃ(LT⁻²)ᵇ gives a + b = 1 and −2b = −1. So b = 1/2 and a = 1/2."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2023","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"Young’s modulus of elasticity Y is expressed in terms of three derived quantities, namely the gravitational constant G, Planck’s constant h and the speed of light c, as Y = c^α h^β G^γ. Which of the following is the correct option?","o":["α = 7, β = −1, γ = −2","α = −7, β = −1, γ = −2","α = 7, β = −1, γ = 2","α = −7, β = 1, γ = −2"],"c":0,"s":"Y = ML⁻¹T⁻². M: β − γ = 1. L: α + 2β + 3γ = −1. T: −α − β − 2γ = −2. Solving gives β = −1, γ = −2, α = 7."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2022","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"In a particular system of units, a physical quantity can be expressed in terms of the electric charge e, the electron mass mₑ, Planck’s constant h, and Coulomb’s constant k = {1;4πε₀}. In terms of these physical constants, the dimension of the magnetic field is [B] = [e]^α [mₑ]^β [h]^γ [k]^δ. The value of α + β + γ + δ is ______.","n":4,"s":"B = MT⁻¹Q⁻¹, k = ML³T⁻²Q⁻², h = ML²T⁻¹. Charge: α − 2δ = −1. Mass: β + γ + δ = 1. Length: 2γ + 3δ = 0. Time: −γ − 2δ = −1. So δ = 2, γ = −3, β = 2, α = 3, and the sum is 4."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"Assume that the displacement s of air is proportional to the pressure difference ΔP created by a sound wave. The displacement further depends on the speed of sound v, the density of air ρ and the frequency f. If ΔP ~ 10 Pa, v ~ 300 m/s, ρ ~ 1 kg/m³ and f ~ 1000 Hz, then s will be of the order of (take the multiplicative constant to be 1)","o":["3/100 mm","10 mm","1/10 mm","1 mm"],"c":0,"s":"s ∝ ΔP means s = ΔP vᵇρᶜfᵈ. Mass: 1 + c = 0 gives c = −1. Length: −1 + b − 3c = 1 gives b = −1. Time: −2 − b − d = 0 gives d = −1. So s = ΔP/(vρf) = 10/(300 × 1 × 1000) = 3.3 × 10⁻⁵ m = 3/100 mm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 1 (adapted)","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"The stopping potential V₀ in the photoelectric effect is written in units of Planck’s constant h, speed of light c, gravitational constant G and ampere A as V₀ = h^a c^b G^x A⁻¹. Find b.","n":5,"s":"V₀ = ML²T⁻³A⁻¹. Matching M: a − x = 1. L: 2a + b + 3x = 2. T: −a − b − 2x = −3. Solving gives a = 0, b = 5, x = −1. (The original options all fail this check, and its official key marks none as correct, so it is given here as a numerical question.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"The expression for time in terms of G (universal gravitational constant), h (Planck’s constant) and c (speed of light) is proportional to","o":["√{hc⁵;G}","√{Gh;c³}","√{Gh;c⁵}","√{c³;Gh}"],"c":2,"s":"t = Gᵃhᵇcᶜ. Mass: −a + b = 0. Length: 3a + 2b + c = 0. Time: −2a − b − c = 1. Solving gives a = b = 1/2 and c = −5/2, so t ∝ √(Gh/c⁵), the Planck time."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"The characteristic distance at which quantum gravitational effects are significant, the Planck length, can be determined from a suitable combination of the fundamental physical constants G, ħ and c. Which of the following correctly gives the Planck length?","o":["Għ²c³","G²ħc","(Għ/c³)^(1/2)","G^(1/2)ħ²c"],"c":2,"s":"G = M⁻¹L³T⁻², ħ = ML²T⁻¹, c = LT⁻¹. Għ/c³ = L³T⁻²·L²T⁻¹/(L³T⁻³) = L². Its square root is a length."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2016","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"A length-scale l depends on the permittivity ε of a dielectric material, the Boltzmann constant k_B, the absolute temperature T, the number per unit volume n of certain charged particles, and the charge q carried by each of the particles. Which of the following expression(s) for l is (are) dimensionally correct?","o":["l = √({nq²;εk_BT})","l = √({εk_BT;nq²})","l = √({q²;εn^(2/3)k_BT})","l = √({q²;εn^(1/3)k_BT})"],"c":[1,3],"s":"q²/ε has the dimensions of energy × length, so q²/(εk_BT) is a length. Dividing by n^(1/3), which is 1/length, gives length². So (d) works. In (b), εk_BT/(nq²) = 1/(n × length) = length², so it works too. (a) is the inverse of (b), and (c) gives length³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"A beaker contains a fluid of density ρ kg/m³, specific heat S J/kg °C and viscosity η. The beaker is filled up to height h. To estimate the rate of heat transfer per unit area (Q/A) by convection when the beaker is put on a hot plate, a student proposes that it should depend on η, ({SΔθ;h}) and ({1;ρg}), where Δθ (in °C) is the difference in the temperature between the bottom and top of the fluid. In that situation the correct option for (Q/A) is","o":["η{SΔθ;h}","η({SΔθ;h})({1;ρg})","{SΔθ;ηh}","({SΔθ;ηh})({1;ρg})"],"c":0,"s":"Q/A is power per area = MT⁻³. η = ML⁻¹T⁻¹ and SΔθ/h = L²T⁻²/L = LT⁻². Then η·SΔθ/h = ML⁻¹T⁻¹ · LT⁻² = MT⁻³, which matches. The factor 1/(ρg) would spoil it."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2015","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"Planck’s constant h, the speed of light c and the gravitational constant G are used to form a unit of length L and a unit of mass M. Then the correct option(s) is (are)","o":["M ∝ √c","M ∝ √G","L ∝ √h","L ∝ √G"],"c":[0,2,3],"s":"M = √(hc/G) and L = √(hG/c³). So M ∝ √c, L ∝ √h and L ∝ √G are correct. But M ∝ 1/√G, so M ∝ √G is wrong."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2014","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"To find the distance d over which a signal can be seen clearly in foggy conditions, a railways engineer uses dimensional analysis and assumes that the distance depends on the mass density ρ of the fog, the intensity (power/area) S of the light from the signal and its frequency f. The engineer finds that d is proportional to S^(1/n). The value of n is ______.","n":3,"s":"d = ρᵃSᵇfᶜ with S = MT⁻³. Mass: a + b = 0. Length: −3a = 1, so a = −1/3 and b = 1/3. Time: −3b − c = 0 gives c = −1. So d ∝ S^(1/3) and n = 3."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1992","tp":"dims","st":"Deriving relations (powers)","q":"The dimensions of length are expressed as G^x c^y h^z, where G, c and h are the universal gravitational constant, speed of light and Planck’s constant respectively. Then","o":["x = 1/2, y = 1/2","x = 1/2, z = 1/2","y = −3/2, z = 1/2","y = 1/2, z = 3/2"],"c":[1,2],"s":"Length = Gˣcʸhᶻ gives the Planck length √(Għ/c³): x = 1/2, y = −3/2, z = 1/2. So (b) and (c) are correct."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If force (F), velocity (V) and time (T) are considered as fundamental physical quantities, then the dimensional formula of density will be","o":["F²V⁻²T⁶","FV⁻²T²","FV⁴T⁻⁶","FV⁻⁴T⁻²"],"c":3,"s":"F = ma gives M = FT/V, and L = VT. Density = M/L³ = (FT/V)/(VT)³ = FV⁻⁴T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If the velocity of light c, the universal gravitational constant G and Planck’s constant h are chosen as fundamental quantities, the dimensions of mass in the new system is","o":["[h¹c¹G⁻¹]","[h^(−1/2)c^(1/2)G^(1/2)]","[h^(1/2)c^(1/2)G^(−1/2)]","[h^(1/2)c^(−1/2)G¹]"],"c":2,"s":"M = hᵃcᵇGᶜ. Matching M, L and T gives a = 1/2, b = 1/2, c = −1/2. This is the Planck mass √(hc/G)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If momentum [P], area [A] and time [T] are taken as fundamental quantities, then the dimensional formula for the coefficient of viscosity is","o":["[PA⁻¹T⁰]","[PAT⁻¹]","[PA⁻¹T]","[PA⁻¹T⁻¹]"],"c":0,"s":"Viscosity = ML⁻¹T⁻¹. P = MLT⁻¹ and A = L². ML⁻¹T⁻¹ = PˣAʸTᶻ gives x = 1 (mass), 1 + 2y = 0... from L: x + 2y = −1, so y = −1, and T: −x + z = −1, so z = 0. The result is [PA⁻¹T⁰]."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If force (F), length (L) and time (T) are taken as the fundamental quantities, then what will be the dimension of density?","o":["[FL⁻⁴T²]","[FL⁻³T³]","[FL⁻³T²]","[FL⁻⁵T²]"],"c":0,"s":"F = MLT⁻² gives M = FT²/L. Density = M/L³ = FT²L⁻⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If velocity [V], time [T] and force [F] are chosen as the base quantities, the dimensions of the mass will be","o":["[FVT⁻¹]","[FTV⁻¹]","[FT²V]","[FT⁻¹V⁻¹]"],"c":1,"s":"Acceleration = V/T, so M = F/a = FT/V = [FTV⁻¹]."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 20 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If time (t), velocity (v) and angular momentum (l) are taken as the fundamental units, then the dimension of mass (m) in terms of t, v and l is","o":["[t⁻²v⁻¹l¹]","[t⁻¹v¹l⁻²]","[t¹v²l⁻¹]","[t⁻¹v⁻²l¹]"],"c":3,"s":"Length = vt. Angular momentum l = m v r = m v (vt) = m v²t. So m = l v⁻²t⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If speed V, area A and force F are chosen as fundamental units, then the dimension of Young’s modulus will be","o":["FA²V⁻¹","FA²V⁻³","FA²V⁻²","FA⁻¹V⁰"],"c":3,"s":"Young’s modulus has the dimensions of pressure, which is force per area: F A⁻¹ V⁰."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 2 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If momentum (p), area (A) and time (T) are taken to be the fundamental quantities, then the dimensional formula for energy is","o":["[pA^(1/2)T⁻¹]","[pA⁻¹T⁻²]","[p²AT⁻²]","[p^(1/2)AT⁻¹]"],"c":0,"s":"Energy = pᵃAᵇTᶜ. Mass: a = 1. Length: a + 2b = 2, so b = 1/2. Time: −a + c = −2, so c = −1."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2020","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"Sometimes it is convenient to construct a system of units so that all quantities can be expressed in terms of only one physical quantity. In one such system, the dimensions of different quantities are given in terms of a quantity X as follows: [position] = [X^α], [speed] = [X^β], [acceleration] = [X^p], [linear momentum] = [X^q], [force] = [X^r]. Then","o":["α + p = 2β","p + q − r = β","p − q + r = α","p + q + r = β"],"c":[0,1],"s":"Time = position/speed = X^(α − β). Acceleration = speed/time gives p = β − (α − β) = 2β − α, so α + p = 2β. Force = momentum/time gives r = q − (α − β). Then p + q − r = (2β − α) + (α − β) = β."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If surface tension (S), moment of inertia (I) and Planck’s constant (h) were to be taken as the fundamental units, the dimensional formula for linear momentum would be","o":["S^(3/2)I^(1/2)h⁰","S^(1/2)I^(1/2)h⁰","S^(1/2)I^(3/2)h⁻¹","S^(1/2)I^(1/2)h⁻¹"],"c":1,"s":"S = MT⁻², I = ML² and h = ML²T⁻¹. For momentum MLT⁻¹ = SᵃIᵇhᶜ: mass gives a + b + c = 1, length gives 2b + 2c = 1, time gives −2a − c = −1. So a = 1/2, c = 0, b = 1/2."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 11 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If speed (V), acceleration (A) and force (F) are considered as fundamental units, the dimension of Young’s modulus will be","o":["V⁻²A²F⁻²","V⁻²A²F²","V⁻⁴A²F","V⁻⁴A⁻²F"],"c":2,"s":"T = V/A, L = V²/A and M = F/A. Young’s modulus = ML⁻¹T⁻² = (F/A)(A/V²)(A²/V²) = F A² V⁻⁴."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2019","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"Let us consider a system of units in which mass and angular momentum are dimensionless. If length has dimension of L, which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?","o":["The dimension of force is L⁻³","The dimension of power is L⁻⁵","The dimension of energy is L⁻²","The dimension of linear momentum is L⁻¹"],"c":[0,2,3],"s":"Angular momentum ML²T⁻¹ is dimensionless and M is dimensionless, so T = L². Force MLT⁻² = L⁻³. Energy ML²T⁻² = L⁻². Momentum MLT⁻¹ = L⁻¹. Power ML²T⁻³ = L⁻⁴, not L⁻⁵."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2017 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"Time (T), velocity (C) and angular momentum (h) are chosen as fundamental quantities instead of mass, length and time. In terms of these, the dimensions of mass would be","o":["[M] = [TC⁻²h]","[M] = [T⁻¹C⁻²h⁻¹]","[M] = [T⁻¹C⁻²h]","[M] = [T⁻¹C²h]"],"c":2,"s":"L = CT. Angular momentum h = ML²T⁻¹ = M C²T²T⁻¹ = M C²T. So M = h C⁻² T⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If the capacitance of a nanocapacitor is measured in terms of a unit u made by combining the electronic charge e, Bohr radius a₀, Planck’s constant h and speed of light c, then","o":["u = {e²c;ha₀}","u = {e²h;ca₀}","u = {e²a₀;hc}","u = {hc;e²a₀}"],"c":2,"s":"Capacitance = charge²/energy. hc/a₀ has the dimensions of energy (h·c is energy × length). So u = e²a₀/(hc)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015 · Online","tp":"dims","st":"New fundamental quantities","q":"If the electronic charge e, electron mass m, speed of light in vacuum c and Planck’s constant h are taken as fundamental quantities, the permeability of vacuum μ₀ can be expressed in units of","o":["({hc;me²})","({h;me²})","({h;ce²})","({mc²;he²})"],"c":2,"s":"The fine-structure constant e²μ₀c/(4πh) has no dimensions, so μ₀ has the dimensions of h/(ce²)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 2 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Coefficient of viscosity  (B) Intensity of wave  (C) Pressure gradient  (D) Compressibility\nList II: (I) [ML⁰T⁻³]  (II) [ML⁻²T⁻²]  (III) [M⁻¹LT²]  (IV) [ML⁻¹T⁻¹]","o":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)"],"c":0,"s":"Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹. Intensity (power per area) is MT⁻³. Pressure gradient is ML⁻¹T⁻² / L = ML⁻²T⁻². Compressibility is 1/pressure = M⁻¹LT²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Gravitational constant  (B) Gravitational potential energy  (C) Gravitational potential  (D) Acceleration due to gravity\nList II: (I) [LT⁻²]  (II) [L²T⁻²]  (III) [ML²T⁻²]  (IV) [M⁻¹L³T⁻²]","o":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"c":0,"s":"G is M⁻¹L³T⁻². Gravitational potential energy is ML²T⁻². Gravitational potential is energy per mass, L²T⁻². Acceleration due to gravity is LT⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Boltzmann constant  (B) Coefficient of viscosity  (C) Planck’s constant  (D) Thermal conductivity\nList II: (I) ML²T⁻¹  (II) MLT⁻³K⁻¹  (III) ML²T⁻²K⁻¹  (IV) ML⁻¹T⁻¹","o":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":0,"s":"Boltzmann constant is energy per kelvin, ML²T⁻²K⁻¹. Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹. Planck’s constant is ML²T⁻¹. Thermal conductivity is MLT⁻³K⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 7 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Mass density  (B) Impulse  (C) Power  (D) Moment of inertia\nList II: (I) [ML²T⁻³]  (II) [MLT⁻¹]  (III) [ML²T⁰]  (IV) [ML⁻³T⁰]","o":["(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"c":1,"s":"Mass density is ML⁻³. Impulse is MLT⁻¹. Power is ML²T⁻³. Moment of inertia is ML²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 28 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Angular impulse  (B) Latent heat  (C) Electrical resistivity  (D) Electromotive force\nList II: (I) [M⁰L²T⁻²]  (II) [ML²T⁻³A⁻¹]  (III) [ML²T⁻¹]  (IV) [ML³T⁻³A⁻²]","o":["(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],"c":2,"s":"Angular impulse is ML²T⁻¹. Latent heat is L²T⁻². Resistivity is ML³T⁻³A⁻². Emf is ML²T⁻³A⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Young’s modulus  (B) Torque  (C) Coefficient of viscosity  (D) Gravitational constant\nList II: (I) ML⁻¹T⁻¹  (II) ML⁻¹T⁻²  (III) M⁻¹L³T⁻²  (IV) ML²T⁻²","o":["(A)-(II), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(I)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"],"c":0,"s":"Young’s modulus is ML⁻¹T⁻². Torque is ML²T⁻². Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹. G is M⁻¹L³T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Torque  (B) Magnetic field  (C) Magnetic moment  (D) Permeability of free space\nList II: (I) [M¹L¹T⁻²A⁻²]  (II) [L²A¹]  (III) [M¹T⁻²A⁻¹]  (IV) [M¹L²T⁻²]","o":["A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"c":3,"s":"Torque is ML²T⁻². Magnetic field is MT⁻²A⁻¹. Magnetic moment (current × area) is L²A. Permeability is MLT⁻²A⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 30 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Coefficient of viscosity  (B) Surface tension  (C) Angular momentum  (D) Rotational kinetic energy\nList II: (I) [ML²T⁻²]  (II) [ML²T⁻¹]  (III) [ML⁻¹T⁻¹]  (IV) [ML⁰T⁻²]","o":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"],"c":1,"s":"Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹. Surface tension is MT⁻². Angular momentum is ML²T⁻¹. Rotational kinetic energy is ML²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Torque  (B) Stress  (C) Pressure gradient  (D) Coefficient of viscosity\nList II: (I) ML⁻²T⁻²  (II) ML²T⁻²  (III) ML⁻¹T⁻¹  (IV) ML⁻¹T⁻²","o":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"c":0,"s":"Torque is ML²T⁻². Stress is ML⁻¹T⁻². Pressure gradient is ML⁻²T⁻². Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 12 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Spring constant  (B) Angular speed  (C) Angular momentum  (D) Moment of inertia\nList II: (I) [T⁻¹]  (II) [MT⁻²]  (III) [ML²]  (IV) [ML²T⁻¹]","o":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"c":2,"s":"Spring constant is force/length = MT⁻². Angular speed is T⁻¹. Angular momentum is ML²T⁻¹. Moment of inertia is ML²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Planck’s constant (h)  (B) Stopping potential (V_s)  (C) Work function (φ)  (D) Momentum (p)\nList II: (I) [M¹L²T⁻²]  (II) [M¹L¹T⁻¹]  (III) [M¹L²T⁻¹]  (IV) [M¹L²T⁻³A⁻¹]","o":["(A)→(II), (B)→(IV), (C)→(III), (D)→(I)","(A)→(III), (B)→(IV), (C)→(I), (D)→(II)","(A)→(I), (B)→(III), (C)→(IV), (D)→(II)","(A)→(III), (B)→(I), (C)→(II), (D)→(IV)"],"c":1,"s":"h is ML²T⁻¹. A potential is ML²T⁻³A⁻¹. A work function is an energy, ML²T⁻². Momentum is MLT⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Surface tension  (B) Pressure  (C) Viscosity  (D) Impulse\nList II: (I) kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹  (II) kg m s⁻¹  (III) kg m⁻¹ s⁻²  (IV) kg s⁻²","o":["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)"],"c":2,"s":"Surface tension is kg s⁻². Pressure is kg m⁻¹ s⁻². Viscosity is kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹. Impulse is kg m s⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 25 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Young’s modulus (Y)  (B) Coefficient of viscosity (η)  (C) Planck’s constant (h)  (D) Work function (φ)\nList II: (I) [ML⁻¹T⁻¹]  (II) [ML²T⁻¹]  (III) [ML⁻¹T⁻²]  (IV) [ML²T⁻²]","o":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"c":3,"s":"Y is ML⁻¹T⁻². η is ML⁻¹T⁻¹. h is ML²T⁻¹. A work function is an energy, ML²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Pressure gradient  (B) Energy density  (C) Electric field  (D) Latent heat\nList II: (I) [M⁰L²T⁻²]  (II) [M¹L⁻¹T⁻²]  (III) [M¹L⁻²T⁻²]  (IV) [M¹L¹T⁻³A⁻¹]","o":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Pressure gradient is ML⁻²T⁻². Energy density is ML⁻¹T⁻². Electric field is MLT⁻³A⁻¹. Latent heat is L²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 30 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Torque  (B) Energy density  (C) Pressure gradient  (D) Impulse\nList II: (I) kg m⁻¹ s⁻²  (II) kg m s⁻¹  (III) kg m⁻² s⁻²  (IV) kg m² s⁻²","o":["A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"c":0,"s":"Torque is kg m² s⁻². Energy density is kg m⁻¹ s⁻². Pressure gradient is kg m⁻² s⁻². Impulse is kg m s⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 31 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Angular momentum  (B) Torque  (C) Stress  (D) Pressure gradient\nList II: (I) [ML²T⁻²]  (II) [ML⁻²T⁻²]  (III) [ML²T⁻¹]  (IV) [ML⁻¹T⁻²]","o":["(A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)","(A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)"],"c":2,"s":"Angular momentum is ML²T⁻¹. Torque is ML²T⁻². Stress is ML⁻¹T⁻². Pressure gradient is ML⁻²T⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Magnetic induction  (B) Magnetic flux  (C) Magnetic permeability  (D) Magnetization\nList II: (i) ML²T⁻²A⁻¹  (ii) M⁰L⁻¹A  (iii) MT⁻²A⁻¹  (iv) MLT⁻²A⁻²","o":["(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(ii)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iv), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(i), (C)-(iv), (D)-(iii)"],"c":1,"s":"Magnetic induction is MT⁻²A⁻¹. Magnetic flux is ML²T⁻²A⁻¹. Permeability is MLT⁻²A⁻². Magnetisation (magnetic moment per volume) is L⁻¹A."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) R_H (Rydberg constant)  (B) h (Planck’s constant)  (C) μ_B (magnetic field energy density)  (D) η (coefficient of viscosity)\nList II: (i) kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹  (ii) kg m² s⁻¹  (iii) m⁻¹  (iv) kg m⁻¹ s⁻²","o":["(A)-(iv), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iii)","(A)-(ii), (B)-(iii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(iv), (D)-(i)","(A)-(iii), (B)-(ii), (C)-(i), (D)-(iv)"],"c":2,"s":"The Rydberg constant is m⁻¹. h is kg m² s⁻¹. Energy density is kg m⁻¹ s⁻². Viscosity is kg m⁻¹ s⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 31 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Torque  (B) Impulse  (C) Tension  (D) Surface tension\nList II: (i) MLT⁻¹  (ii) MT⁻²  (iii) ML²T⁻²  (iv) MLT⁻²","o":["(A)-(iii); (B)-(iv); (C)-(i); (D)-(ii)","(A)-(iii); (B)-(i); (C)-(iv); (D)-(ii)","(A)-(i); (B)-(iii); (C)-(iv); (D)-(ii)","(A)-(ii); (B)-(i); (C)-(iv); (D)-(iii)"],"c":1,"s":"Torque is ML²T⁻². Impulse is MLT⁻¹. Tension is a force, MLT⁻². Surface tension is MT⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) Capacitance, C  (B) Permittivity of free space, ε₀  (C) Permeability of free space, μ₀  (D) Electric field, E\nList II: (i) M¹L¹T⁻³A⁻¹  (ii) M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A²  (iii) M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A²  (iv) M¹L¹T⁻²A⁻²","o":["(A)→(iv), (B)→(ii), (C)→(iii), (D)→(i)","(A)→(iii), (B)→(iv), (C)→(ii), (D)→(i)","(A)→(iii), (B)→(ii), (C)→(iv), (D)→(i)","(A)→(iv), (B)→(iii), (C)→(ii), (D)→(i)"],"c":2,"s":"Capacitance is M⁻¹L⁻²T⁴A². ε₀ is M⁻¹L⁻³T⁴A². μ₀ is MLT⁻²A⁻². Electric field is MLT⁻³A⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 25 Feb · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I: (A) h (Planck’s constant)  (B) E (kinetic energy)  (C) V (electric potential)  (D) P (linear momentum)\nList II: (i) [MLT⁻¹]  (ii) [ML²T⁻¹]  (iii) [ML²T⁻²]  (iv) [ML²I⁻¹T⁻³]","o":["(A)→(ii), (B)→(iii), (C)→(iv), (D)→(i)","(A)→(iii), (B)→(ii), (C)→(iv), (D)→(i)","(A)→(iii), (B)→(iv), (C)→(ii), (D)→(i)","(A)→(i), (B)→(ii), (C)→(iv), (D)→(iii)"],"c":0,"s":"h is ML²T⁻¹. Kinetic energy is ML²T⁻². Potential is ML²I⁻¹T⁻³. Momentum is MLT⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2013","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match List I with List II and select the correct answer using the codes.\nList I: P. Boltzmann constant  Q. Coefficient of viscosity  R. Planck constant  S. Thermal conductivity\nList II: 1. [ML²T⁻¹]  2. [ML⁻¹T⁻¹]  3. [MLT⁻³K⁻¹]  4. [ML²T⁻²K⁻¹]","o":["P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3"],"c":2,"s":"The Boltzmann constant is energy per kelvin, ML²T⁻²K⁻¹ (4). Viscosity is ML⁻¹T⁻¹ (2). Planck’s constant is ML²T⁻¹ (1). Thermal conductivity is MLT⁻³K⁻¹ (3)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2007","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Some physical quantities are given in column I and some possible SI units in which these quantities may be expressed are given in column II. Match them.\n(a) GM_eM_s (G: gravitational constant, M_e: mass of Earth, M_s: mass of Sun)\n(b) 3RT/M (R: gas constant, T: temperature, M: molar mass)\n(c) F²/(q²B²) (F: force, q: charge, B: magnetic field)\n(d) GM_e/R_e (R_e: radius of Earth)\nColumn II: (p) (volt)(coulomb)(metre)  (q) (kilogram)(metre)³(second)⁻²  (r) (metre)²(second)⁻²  (s) (farad)(volt)²(kilogram)⁻¹","a":"(a) → p, q   (b) → r, s   (c) → r, s   (d) → r, s","s":"(a) G·M_e·M_s has the unit kg m³ s⁻², the same as volt·coulomb·metre (joule·metre). (b) 3RT/M is energy per mass, m² s⁻², and farad·volt²/kg is also joule per kg. (c) F/(qB) is a speed, so its square is m² s⁻². (d) GM_e/R_e is energy per mass, m² s⁻², the same as (r) and (s)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1990","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Column I gives three physical quantities. Select the appropriate units for them from column II. Some quantities may have more than one choice correct.\n(a) Capacitance  (b) Inductance  (c) Magnetic induction\nColumn II: (p) ohm-second  (q) coulomb²-joule⁻¹  (r) coulomb (volt)⁻¹  (s) newton (ampere metre)⁻¹  (t) volt-second (ampere)⁻¹","a":"(a) → q, r   (b) → p, t   (c) → s","s":"Capacitance = charge/volt = coulomb/volt, and also coulomb²/joule. Inductance = volt·second/ampere = ohm·second. Magnetic induction B = F/(I·l) = newton/(ampere·metre)."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 1983","tp":"dims","st":"Matching quantities with dimensions","q":"Match the physical quantities in column I with dimensions expressed in terms of mass (M), length (L), time (T) and charge (Q) in column II.\n(a) Angular momentum  (b) Torque  (c) Inductance  (d) Latent heat  (e) Capacitance  (f) Resistivity\nColumn II: (p) [ML²T⁻²]  (q) [ML²T⁻¹]  (r) [M⁻¹L⁻²T²Q²]  (s) [ML²Q⁻²]  (t) [ML³T⁻¹Q⁻²]  (u) [L²T⁻²]","a":"(a) → q   (b) → p   (c) → s   (d) → u   (e) → r   (f) → t","s":"Angular momentum ML²T⁻¹. Torque ML²T⁻². Inductance ML²Q⁻². Latent heat L²T⁻². Capacitance M⁻¹L⁻²T²Q². Resistivity ML³T⁻¹Q⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"dims","st":"Statements and assertion-reason","q":"Statement I: Dimensions of specific heat is [L²T⁻²K⁻¹].\nStatement II: Dimensions of gas constant is [ML²T⁻¹K⁻¹].\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer.","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"c":3,"s":"Specific heat is energy/(mass × kelvin) = L²T⁻²K⁻¹, so Statement I is correct. The gas constant is energy/(mole × kelvin) = ML²T⁻²K⁻¹ per mole, not ML²T⁻¹K⁻¹, so Statement II is incorrect."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 27 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Statements and assertion-reason","q":"Statement I: Planck’s constant and angular momentum have the same dimensions.\nStatement II: Linear momentum and moment of force have the same dimensions.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"c":1,"s":"Both h and angular momentum are ML²T⁻¹, so Statement I is true. Linear momentum is MLT⁻¹ and the moment of a force (torque) is ML²T⁻², so Statement II is false."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 29 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Statements and assertion-reason","q":"Assertion (A): The time period of oscillation of a liquid drop depends on the surface tension S. If the density of the liquid is ρ and the radius of the drop is r, then T = K√{ρr³;S^(3/2)} is dimensionally correct, where K is dimensionless.\nReason (R): Using dimensional analysis we get the R.H.S. having a different dimension than that of the time period.","o":["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],"c":3,"s":"[ρr³] = M and [S^(3/2)] = M^(3/2)T⁻³, so the root has the dimensions M^(−1/4)T^(3/2), which is not time. So (A) is false and (R) is true. The correct form is T = K√(ρr³/S)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"dims","st":"Statements and assertion-reason","q":"Assertion A: The product of pressure (P) and time (t) has the same dimension as the coefficient of viscosity.\nReason R: Coefficient of viscosity = {Force;Velocity gradient}.","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"c":2,"s":"P·t = ML⁻¹T⁻² × T = ML⁻¹T⁻¹, the dimensions of viscosity, so A is true. But viscosity is force per area divided by velocity gradient. The Reason leaves out the area, so R is false."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2017","tp":"dims","st":"Other problems","q":"Consider an expanding sphere of instantaneous radius R whose total mass remains constant. The expansion is such that the instantaneous density ρ remains uniform throughout the volume. The rate of fractional change in density {1;ρ}{dρ;dt} is constant. The velocity v of any point on the surface of the expanding sphere is proportional to","o":["R","1/R","R^(2/3)","R³"],"c":0,"s":"Mass is constant, so ρ ∝ 1/R³ and (1/ρ)(dρ/dt) = −3(1/R)(dR/dt) = constant. So dR/dt ∝ R, which means v ∝ R."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A quantity Q is formulated as X^(−2) Y^(3/2) Z^(−2/5). X, Y and Z are independent parameters with fractional errors of 0.1, 0.2 and 0.5 respectively. The maximum fractional error of Q is","o":["0.1","0.8","0.6","0.7"],"c":3,"s":"Each fractional error is multiplied by the size of its power and all are added. 2(0.1) + (3/2)(0.2) + (2/5)(0.5) = 0.2 + 0.3 + 0.2 = 0.7."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 23 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The energy of a system is given as E(t) = α³ e^(−βt), where t is the time and β = 0.3 s⁻¹. The errors in the measurement of α and t are 1.2 % and 1.6 % respectively. At t = 5 s, the maximum percentage error in the energy is","o":["8.4 %","11.6 %","6 %","4 %"],"c":2,"s":"ΔE/E = 3(Δα/α) + βΔt. βΔt = βt(Δt/t) = (0.3)(5)(1.6 %) = 2.4 %. Total = 3(1.2 %) + 2.4 % = 6 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity C is related to four other quantities p, q, r and s as C = {pq²;r³√s}. The percentage errors in the measurement of p, q, r and s are 1 %, 2 %, 3 % and 2 % respectively. The percentage error in the measurement of C will be ______ %.","n":15,"s":"1 + 2(2) + 3(3) + ½(2) = 1 + 4 + 9 + 1 = 15 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity Q is related to four observables a, b, c, d as Q = {ab⁴;cd}, where a = (60 ± 3) Pa, b = (20 ± 0.1) m, c = (40 ± 0.2) N s m⁻² and d = (50 ± 0.1) m. The percentage error in Q is x/1000. Then x = ______.","n":7700,"s":"ΔQ/Q = 3/60 + 4(0.1/20) + 0.2/40 + 0.1/50 = 5 % + 2 % + 0.5 % + 0.2 % = 7.7 % = 7700/1000, so x = 7700."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"To find the spring constant k of a spring experimentally, a student commits a 2 % positive error in the measurement of time and a 1 % negative error in the measurement of mass. The percentage error in determining the value of k is","o":["4 %","1 %","3 %","5 %"],"c":3,"s":"k = 4π²m/T². Δk/k = Δm/m − 2ΔT/T = (−1 %) − 2(+2 %) = −5 %, a 5 % error."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity Q depends on quantities a, b, c by the relation Q = {a⁴b³;c²}. The percentage errors in a, b and c are 3 %, 4 % and 5 % respectively. Then the percentage error in Q is","o":["14 %","43 %","34 %","66 %"],"c":2,"s":"4(3 %) + 3(4 %) + 2(5 %) = 12 + 12 + 10 = 34 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 10 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity P is given as P = {a²b³;c√d}. The percentage errors in the measurement of a, b, c and d are 1 %, 2 %, 3 % and 4 % respectively. The percentage error in the measurement of P will be","o":["13 %","12 %","14 %","16 %"],"c":0,"s":"2(1) + 3(2) + 3 + (1/2)(4) = 2 + 6 + 3 + 2 = 13 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 13 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A body of mass (5 ± 0.5) kg is moving with a velocity of (20 ± 0.4) m/s. Its kinetic energy will be","o":["(1000 ± 140) J","(1000 ± 0.14) J","(500 ± 0.14) J","(500 ± 140) J"],"c":0,"s":"K = ½mv² = ½(5)(400) = 1000 J. ΔK/K = 0.5/5 + 2(0.4/20) = 0.1 + 0.04 = 0.14, so ΔK = 140 J."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The maximum error in the measurement of resistance, current and time for which current flows in an electrical circuit are 1 %, 2 % and 3 % respectively. The maximum percentage error in the detection of the dissipated heat will be","o":["2","4","6","8"],"c":3,"s":"H = I²Rt. ΔH/H = 2(2 %) + 1 % + 3 % = 8 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jul · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A torque meter is calibrated to reference standards of mass, length and time, each with 5 % accuracy. After calibration, the measured torque with this torque meter will have a net accuracy of","o":["15 %","25 %","75 %","5 %"],"c":1,"s":"Torque has dimensions ML²T⁻². Error = 5 % + 2(5 %) + 2(5 %) = 25 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"If Z = {A²B³;C⁴}, then the relative error in Z will be","o":["ΔA/A + ΔB/B + ΔC/C","2ΔA/A + 3ΔB/B − 4ΔC/C","2ΔA/A + 3ΔB/B + 4ΔC/C","ΔA/A + ΔB/B − ΔC/C"],"c":2,"s":"Each relative error is multiplied by its power and they all add, even for a quotient. ΔZ/Z = 2ΔA/A + 3ΔB/B + 4ΔC/C."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 25 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"For z = a²x³y^(1/2), where a is a constant, the percentage errors in the measurement of x and y are 4 % and 12 % respectively. Then the percentage error in z will be ______ %.","n":18,"s":"The constant a has no error. 3(4 %) + ½(12 %) = 12 % + 6 % = 18 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Jul · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity y is represented by the formula y = m² r⁻⁴ g^x l^(−3/2). If the percentage errors found in y, m, r, l and g are 18, 1, 0.5, 4 and p respectively, then find the value of x and p.","o":["5 and ±2","4 and ±3","8 and ±2","16/3 and ±3/2"],"c":3,"s":"18 = 2(1) + 4(0.5) + (3/2)(4) + x·p, so x·p = 8. Only 16/3 × 3/2 = 8 fits."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The acceleration due to gravity is found up to an accuracy of 4 % on a planet. The energy supplied to a simple pendulum of known mass m to undertake oscillations of time period T is being estimated. If the time period is measured to an accuracy of 3 %, the accuracy to which E is known is ______ %.","n":14,"s":"The energy supplied is E = mgL(1 − cos θ) and L = gT²/4π², so E ∝ g²T². Error = 2(4 %) + 2(3 %) = 14 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The radius of a sphere is measured to be (7.50 ± 0.85) cm. Suppose the percentage error in its volume is x. The value of x, to the nearest integer, is ______.","n":34,"s":"Δr/r = 0.85/7.50 = 11.33 %. V ∝ r³, so ΔV/V = 3(11.33 %) = 34 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 5 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity z depends on four observables a, b, c and d, as z = {a²b^(2/3);√c d³}. The percentages of error in the measurement of a, b, c and d are 2 %, 1.5 %, 4 % and 2.5 % respectively. The percentage of error in z is","o":["12.25 %","16.5 %","13.5 %","14.5 %"],"c":3,"s":"2(2 %) + (2/3)(1.5 %) + ½(4 %) + 3(2.5 %) = 4 + 1 + 2 + 7.5 = 14.5 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The relative error in the determination of the surface area of a sphere is α. Then the relative error in the determination of its volume is","o":["(3/2)α","α","(5/2)α","(2/3)α"],"c":0,"s":"Area ∝ r², so the relative error in r is α/2. Volume ∝ r³, so its relative error is 3(α/2) = (3/2)α."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The percentage errors in the quantities P, Q, R and S are 0.5 %, 1 %, 3 % and 1.5 % respectively in the measurement of a physical quantity A = {P³Q²;√R S}. The maximum percentage error in the value of A will be","o":["6.5 %","8.5 %","6.0 %","7.5 %"],"c":0,"s":"3(0.5) + 2(1) + ½(3) + 1.5 = 1.5 + 2 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 6.5 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The relative uncertainty in the period of a satellite orbiting around the Earth is 10⁻². If the relative uncertainty in the radius of the orbit is negligible, the relative uncertainty in the mass of the Earth is","o":["6 × 10⁻²","10⁻²","2 × 10⁻²","3 × 10⁻²"],"c":2,"s":"T² = 4π²r³/(GM), so M = 4π²r³/(GT²). With r exact, ΔM/M = 2ΔT/T = 2 × 10⁻²."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2017 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"A physical quantity P is described by the relation P = a^(1/2) b² c³ d^(−4). If the relative errors in the measurement of a, b, c and d are 2 %, 1 %, 3 % and 5 % respectively, then the relative error in P will be","o":["25 %","12 %","8 %","32 %"],"c":3,"s":"½(2) + 2(1) + 3(3) + 4(5) = 1 + 2 + 9 + 20 = 32 %."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2015","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The energy of a system as a function of time t is given as E(t) = A² exp(−αt), where α = 0.2 s⁻¹. The measurement of A has an error of 1.25 %. If the error in the measurement of time is 1.50 %, the percentage error in the value of E(t) at t = 5 s is","n":4,"s":"ΔE/E = 2(1.25 %) + αΔt = 2.5 % + (0.2)(5)(1.5 %) = 2.5 % + 1.5 % = 4 %."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2012 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Error in a power-law quantity","q":"The amount of heat produced in an electric circuit depends upon the current (I), the resistance (R) and the time (t). If the errors made in the measurements of the above quantities are 2 %, 1 % and 1 % respectively, then the maximum possible error in the total heat produced will be","o":["1 %","2 %","6 %","3 %"],"c":2,"s":"H = I²Rt. ΔH/H = 2(2 %) + 1 % + 1 % = 6 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 22 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"The maximum percentage error in the measurement of the density of a wire is [mass of wire = (0.60 ± 0.003) g, radius of wire = (0.50 ± 0.01) cm, length of wire = (10.00 ± 0.05) cm]","o":["5","8","4","7"],"c":0,"s":"ρ = m/(πr²L). Δρ/ρ = Δm/m + 2Δr/r + ΔL/L = 0.5 % + 2(2 %) + 0.5 % = 5 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"A cylindrical wire of mass (0.4 ± 0.01) g has length (8 ± 0.04) cm and radius (6 ± 0.03) mm. The maximum error in its density will be","o":["3.5 %","1 %","4 %","5 %"],"c":2,"s":"ρ = m/(πr²L). Δρ/ρ = 0.01/0.4 + 2(0.03/6) + 0.04/8 = 2.5 % + 1 % + 0.5 % = 4 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"A silver wire has a mass (0.6 ± 0.006) g, radius (0.5 ± 0.005) mm and length (4 ± 0.04) cm. The maximum percentage error in the measurement of its density will be","o":["4 %","3 %","6 %","7 %"],"c":0,"s":"Each of m, r and L has a 1 % error. Δρ/ρ = 1 % + 2(1 %) + 1 % = 4 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"The density of a solid metal sphere is determined by measuring its mass and its diameter. The maximum error in the density of the sphere is (x/100) %. If the relative errors in measuring the mass and the diameter are 6.0 % and 1.5 % respectively, the value of x is ______.","n":1050,"s":"ρ = 6m/(πD³). Δρ/ρ = 6.0 % + 3(1.5 %) = 10.5 % = 1050/100 %, so x = 1050."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 9 Apr · Shift 1 (adapted)","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"In the density measurement of a cube, the mass and edge length are measured as (10.00 ± 0.10) kg and (0.10 ± 0.01) m respectively. Find the percentage error in the measured density.","n":31,"s":"ρ = m/a³. Δρ/ρ = 0.10/10.00 + 3(0.01/0.10) = 1 % + 30 % = 31 %. (The original question asked for the error in kg/m³ and its official key marks no option as correct, so it is given here as a percentage.)"},
{"y":"JEE Main 2018","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"The density of a material in the shape of a cube is determined by measuring three sides of the cube and its mass. If the relative errors in measuring the mass and length are respectively 1.5 % and 1 %, the maximum error in determining the density is","o":["2.5 %","3.5 %","4.5 %","6 %"],"c":2,"s":"ρ = m/L³. Δρ/ρ = 1.5 % + 3(1 %) = 4.5 %."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2004","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: density","q":"A wire of length l = 6 ± 0.06 cm and radius r = 0.5 ± 0.005 cm has mass m = 0.3 ± 0.003 g. The maximum percentage error in its density is","o":["4","2","1","6.8"],"c":0,"s":"ρ = m/(πr²l). Δρ/ρ = 1 % + 2(1 %) + 1 % = 4 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 29 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"The resistance R = {V;I}, where V = (200 ± 5) V and I = (20 ± 0.2) A. The percentage error in the measurement of R is","o":["5.5 %","7 %","3 %","3.5 %"],"c":3,"s":"ΔR/R = ΔV/V + ΔI/I = 5/200 + 0.2/20 = 2.5 % + 1 % = 3.5 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 31 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"If the percentage errors in measuring the length and the diameter of a wire are 0.1 % each, the percentage error in measuring its resistance will be","o":["0.3 %","0.2 %","0.1 %","0.144 %"],"c":0,"s":"R = 4ρL/(πd²). ΔR/R = ΔL/L + 2Δd/d = 0.1 % + 2(0.1 %) = 0.3 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 6 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"Two resistances are given as R₁ = (10 ± 0.5) Ω and R₂ = (15 ± 0.5) Ω. The percentage error in the measurement of the equivalent resistance when they are connected in parallel is","o":["6.33","2.33","4.33","5.33"],"c":2,"s":"R = R₁R₂/(R₁ + R₂) = 6 Ω. ΔR = R²(ΔR₁/R₁² + ΔR₂/R₂²) = 36(0.005 + 0.00222) = 0.26 Ω. ΔR/R = 0.26/6 = 4.33 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 1 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"Two resistors R₁ = (4 ± 0.8) Ω and R₂ = (4 ± 0.4) Ω are connected in parallel. The equivalent resistance of their parallel combination will be","o":["(4 ± 0.4) Ω","(2 ± 0.4) Ω","(4 ± 0.3) Ω","(2 ± 0.3) Ω"],"c":3,"s":"R = R₁R₂/(R₁ + R₂) = 2 Ω. ΔR = R²(ΔR₁/R₁² + ΔR₂/R₂²) = 4(0.8/16 + 0.4/16) = 0.3 Ω."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 16 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"The resistance R = {V;I}, where V = (50 ± 2) V and I = (20 ± 0.2) A. The percentage error in R is x %. The value of x to the nearest integer is ______.","n":5,"s":"ΔR/R = 2/50 + 0.2/20 = 4 % + 1 % = 5 %."},
{"y":"AIEEE 2012","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: resistance","q":"The resistance of a given wire is obtained by measuring the current flowing in it and the voltage difference applied across it. If the percentage errors in the measurement of the current and the voltage difference are 3 % each, then the error in the value of the resistance of the wire is","o":["zero","1 %","3 %","6 %"],"c":3,"s":"R = V/I. The errors add: ΔR/R = 3 % + 3 % = 6 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 26 Aug · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"If the length of the pendulum in a pendulum clock increases by 0.1 %, then the error in time per day is","o":["43.2 s","8.64 s","86.4 s","4.42 s"],"c":0,"s":"T ∝ √l, so ΔT/T = ½(0.1 %) = 0.05 %. In a day of 86400 s the error is 0.0005 × 86400 = 43.2 s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 18 Mar · Shift 1","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"The time period of a simple pendulum is given by T = 2π√{l;g}. The measured value of the length of the pendulum is 10 cm known to a 1 mm accuracy. The time for 200 oscillations of the pendulum is found to be 100 s using a clock of 1 s resolution. The percentage accuracy in the determination of g using this pendulum is x. The value of x to the nearest integer is","o":["3 %","4 %","5 %","2 %"],"c":0,"s":"g = 4π²l/T². Δl/l = 0.1/10 = 1 %. ΔT/T = Δt/t = 1/100 = 1 %. Δg/g = 1 % + 2(1 %) = 3 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 24 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is T = 2π√{L;g}. The measured value of L is 1.0 m from a metre scale having a minimum division of 1 mm, and the time of one complete oscillation is 1.95 s measured from a stopwatch of 0.01 s resolution. The percentage error in the determination of g will be","o":["1.03 %","1.30 %","1.13 %","1.33 %"],"c":2,"s":"ΔL/L = 0.001/1.0 = 0.1 %. ΔT/T = 0.01/1.95 = 0.513 %. Δg/g = 0.1 % + 2(0.513 %) = 1.13 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 8 Jan · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"A simple pendulum is used to determine the value of gravitational acceleration g at a certain place. The length of the pendulum is 25.0 cm and a stopwatch with 1 s resolution measures the time taken for 40 oscillations to be 50 s. The accuracy in g is","o":["2.40 %","5.40 %","4.40 %","3.40 %"],"c":2,"s":"g = 4π²l/T². Δl/l = 0.1/25.0 = 0.4 %. ΔT/T = 1/50 = 2 %. Δg/g = 0.4 % + 2(2 %) = 4.4 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 8 Apr · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"In a simple pendulum experiment for the determination of the acceleration due to gravity g, the time taken for 20 oscillations is measured with a watch of 1 second least count. The mean value of the time comes out to be 30 s. The length of the pendulum is measured with a metre scale of least count 1 mm and the value obtained is 55.0 cm. The percentage error in the determination of g is close to","o":["0.7 %","6.8 %","0.2 %","3.5 %"],"c":1,"s":"Δl/l = 0.1/55.0 = 0.18 %. ΔT/T = 1/30 = 3.33 %. Δg/g = 0.18 % + 2(3.33 %) = 6.8 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2015","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is T = 2π√{L;g}. The measured value of L is 20.0 cm known to 1 mm accuracy, and the time for 100 oscillations of the pendulum is found to be 90 s using a wrist watch of 1 s resolution. The accuracy in the determination of g is","o":["1 %","5 %","2 %","3 %"],"c":3,"s":"ΔL/L = 0.1/20.0 = 0.5 %. ΔT/T = 1/90 = 1.1 %. Δg/g = 0.5 % + 2(1.1 %) = 2.7 %, about 3 %."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2014 · Online","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"An experiment is performed to obtain the value of the acceleration due to gravity g by using a simple pendulum of length L. In this experiment the time for 100 oscillations is measured with a watch of 1 second least count and the value is 90.0 s. The length L is measured with a metre scale of least count 1 mm and the value is 20.0 cm. The error in the determination of g would be","o":["1.7 %","2.7 %","4.4 %","2.27 %"],"c":1,"s":"ΔL/L = 0.1/20.0 = 0.5 %. ΔT/T = 1/90.0 = 1.11 %. Δg/g = 0.5 % + 2(1.11 %) = 2.7 %."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2010","tp":"err","st":"Experiments: pendulum and time period","q":"A student uses a simple pendulum of exactly 1 m length to determine g, the acceleration due to gravity. He uses a stopwatch with a least count of 1 s and records 40 s for 20 oscillations. For this observation, which of the following statement(s) is (are) true?","o":["The error ΔT in measuring T, the time period, is 0.05 s","The error ΔT in measuring T, the time period, is 1 s","The percentage error in the determination of g is 5 %","The percentage error in the determination of g is 2.5 %"],"c":[0,2],"s":"T = 40/20 = 2 s and Δt = 1 s, so ΔT = 1/20 = 0.05 s. ΔT/T = 2.5 %. Δg/g = 2(2.5 %) = 5 % (the length is exact)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Mean and absolute error","q":"A student in the laboratory measures the thickness of a wire using a screw gauge. The readings are 1.22 mm, 1.23 mm, 1.19 mm and 1.20 mm. The percentage error is x/121 %. The value of x is ______.","n":150,"s":"Mean = 1.21 mm. Absolute deviations: 0.01, 0.02, 0.02, 0.01, so the mean absolute error = 0.015 mm. Percentage error = 0.015/1.21 × 100 = 150/121 %, so x = 150."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2020 · 6 Sep · Shift 2","tp":"err","st":"Mean and absolute error","q":"A student measuring the diameter of a pencil of circular cross-section with a vernier scale records four readings: 5.50 mm, 5.55 mm, 5.54 mm and 5.65 mm. The average of these four readings is 5.5375 mm and the standard deviation of the data is 0.07395 mm. The average diameter of the pencil should therefore be recorded as","o":["(5.5375 ± 0.0739) mm","(5.5375 ± 0.0740) mm","(5.538 ± 0.074) mm","(5.54 ± 0.07) mm"],"c":3,"s":"The error 0.07395 has one significant figure when quoted as 0.07, so the mean is rounded to the same decimal place: 5.54 ± 0.07 mm."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2016","tp":"err","st":"Mean and absolute error","q":"A student measures the time period of 100 oscillations of a simple pendulum four times. The data set is 90 s, 91 s, 95 s and 92 s. If the minimum division in the measuring clock is 1 s, then the reported mean time should be","o":["92 ± 2 s","92 ± 5.0 s","92 ± 1.8 s","92 ± 3 s"],"c":0,"s":"Mean = 92 s. Deviations are 2, 1, 3, 0, so the mean absolute error = 6/4 = 1.5 s. The least count is 1 s, so the error is quoted to the same precision: 92 ± 2 s."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2018","tp":"err","st":"Other error problems","q":"If the measurement errors in all the independent quantities are known, the error in any dependent quantity can be found by series expansion truncated at the first power of the error (Δx/x ≪ 1). Relative errors in independent variables are always added.\nConsider the ratio r = {1 − a;1 + a} to be determined by measuring a dimensionless quantity a. If the error in the measurement of a is Δa (Δa/a ≪ 1), then what is the error Δr in determining r?","o":["{Δa;(1 + a)²}","{2Δa;(1 + a)²}","{2Δa;1 − a²}","{2aΔa;1 − a²}"],"c":1,"s":"dr/da = [−(1 + a) − (1 − a)]/(1 + a)² = −2/(1 + a)². So the size of the error is Δr = 2Δa/(1 + a)²."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2018","tp":"err","st":"Other error problems","q":"In an experiment the initial number of radioactive nuclei is 3000. It is found that 1000 ± 40 nuclei decayed in the first 1.0 s. For |x| ≪ 1, ln(1 + x) = x up to first power in x. The error Δλ, in the determination of the decay constant λ, in s⁻¹, is","o":["0.04","0.03","0.02","0.01"],"c":2,"s":"The nuclei left are N = 3000 − (1000 ± 40) = 2000 ∓ 40. λ = ln(N₀/N) per second, so Δλ = ΔN/N = 40/2000 = 0.02 s⁻¹."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2017","tp":"err","st":"Other error problems","q":"A person measures the depth of a well by measuring the time interval between dropping a stone and receiving the sound of impact with the bottom of the well. The error in his measurement of time is δT = 0.01 s and he measures the depth of the well to be L = 20 m. Take g = 10 m s⁻² and the velocity of sound as 300 m s⁻¹. Then the fractional error in the measurement, δL/L, is closest to","o":["5 %","1 %","3 %","0.2 %"],"c":1,"s":"T = √(2L/g) + L/v. dT/dL = 1/√(2gL) + 1/v = 1/20 + 1/300 = 0.0533. δL = 0.01/0.0533 = 0.1875 m. δL/L = 0.94 %, closest to 1 %."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2016","tp":"err","st":"Other error problems","q":"In an experiment to determine the acceleration due to gravity g, the formula used for the time period of a periodic motion is T = 2π√{7(R − r);5g}. The values of R and r are measured to be (60 ± 1) mm and (10 ± 1) mm respectively. In five successive measurements, the time period is found to be 0.52 s, 0.56 s, 0.57 s, 0.54 s and 0.59 s. The least count of the watch used for the measurement of time period is 0.01 s. Which of the following statement(s) is (are) true?","o":["The error in the measurement of r is 10 %","The error in the measurement of T is 3.57 %","The error in the measurement of T is 2 %","The error in the determined value of g is 11 %"],"c":[0,1,3],"s":"r: 1/10 = 10 %. Mean T = 0.556 s and the mean absolute deviation = 0.0208 s, quoted as 0.02 s to the least count. ΔT/T = 0.02/0.56 = 3.57 %. g ∝ (R − r)/T², so Δg/g = (1 + 1)/50 + 2(3.57 %) = 4 % + 7.14 % ≈ 11 %."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2013","tp":"err","st":"Other error problems","q":"Using the expression 2d sin θ = λ, one calculates the values of d by measuring the corresponding angles θ in the range 0 to 90°. The wavelength λ is exactly known and the error in θ is constant for all values of θ. As θ increases from 0°,","o":["the absolute error in d remains constant","the absolute error in d increases","the fractional error in d remains constant","the fractional error in d decreases"],"c":3,"s":"d = λ/(2 sin θ). Δd/d = cot θ · Δθ, which falls as θ increases, so the fractional error decreases. The absolute error Δd = (λ/2)(cos θ/sin²θ)Δθ also falls, so options a and b are wrong."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 1 Feb · Shift 2","tp":"sig","st":"Counting significant figures","q":"Match List I with List II.\nList I (number): (A) 1001  (B) 010.1  (C) 100.100  (D) 0.0010010\nList II (significant figures): (I) 3  (II) 4  (III) 5  (IV) 6","o":["(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],"c":0,"s":"1001 has 4. 010.1 has 3 because the leading zero does not count. 100.100 has 6 because the trailing zeros after the decimal count. 0.0010010 has 5 (the digits 1, 0, 0, 1, 0)."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 3 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"sig","st":"Adding, multiplying and rounding","q":"A person measures the mass of 3 different particles as 435.42 g, 226.3 g and 0.125 g. According to the rules for arithmetic operations with significant figures, the addition of the masses of the 3 particles will be","o":["661.8 g","661.845 g","661.84 g","662 g"],"c":0,"s":"The sum is 661.845 g. In addition keep the fewest decimal places. 226.3 g has one, so the answer is 661.8 g."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2025 · 24 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"sig","st":"Adding, multiplying and rounding","q":"For an experimental expression y = {32.3 × 1125;27.4}, where all the digits are significant, to report the value of y we should write","o":["y = 1326.2","y = 1330","y = 1326.186","y = 1326.19"],"c":1,"s":"The data have 3, 4 and 3 significant figures, so the answer has 3. 1326.186 rounds to 1330."},
{"y":"JEE Advanced 2025","tp":"sig","st":"Adding, multiplying and rounding","q":"The length, breadth and thickness of a strip having a uniform cross-section are measured to be 10.5 cm, 0.05 mm and 6.0 µm respectively. Which of the following option(s) give(s) the volume of the strip in cm³ with the correct significant figures?","o":["3.2 × 10⁻⁵","32.0 × 10⁻⁶","3.0 × 10⁻⁵","3 × 10⁻⁵"],"c":3,"s":"Volume = 10.5 × 0.005 × 6.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.15 × 10⁻⁵ cm³. The breadth 0.05 mm has only one significant figure, so the volume must have one: 3 × 10⁻⁵ cm³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 5 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"sig","st":"Adding, multiplying and rounding","q":"Time periods of oscillation of the same simple pendulum measured using four different clocks were recorded as 4.62 s, 4.632 s, 4.6 s and 4.64 s. The arithmetic mean of these readings in the correct significant figures is","o":["4.6 s","4.62 s","5 s","4.623 s"],"c":0,"s":"The mean is 4.623 s. The least precise reading, 4.6 s, has one decimal place, so the mean is written 4.6 s."},
{"y":"IIT-JEE 2003","tp":"sig","st":"Adding, multiplying and rounding","q":"A cube has a side of length 1.2 × 10⁻² m. Calculate its volume.","o":["1.7 × 10⁻⁶ m³","1.73 × 10⁻⁶ m³","1.70 × 10⁻⁶ m³","1.732 × 10⁻⁶ m³"],"c":0,"s":"(1.2 × 10⁻²)³ = 1.728 × 10⁻⁶ m³. The side has two significant figures, so the volume is 1.7 × 10⁻⁶ m³."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 28 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"units","st":"Changing units","q":"Velocity (v) and acceleration (a) in two systems of units 1 and 2 are related as v₂ = {n;m²}v₁ and a₂ = {a₁;mn} respectively. Here m and n are constants. The relations for distance and time in the two systems respectively are","o":["{n³;m³}L₁ = L₂ and {n²;m}T₁ = T₂","L₁ = {n⁴;m²}L₂ and T₁ = {n²;m}T₂","L₁ = {n²;m}L₂ and T₁ = {n⁴;m²}T₂","{n²;m}L₁ = L₂ and {n⁴;m²}T₁ = T₂"],"c":0,"s":"a/v has dimensions of 1/T. So 1/T₂ = (a₂/v₂) = (a₁/v₁)(m/n²), which gives T₂ = (n²/m)T₁. Then L₂ = v₂T₂ = (n/m²)(L₁/T₁)(n²/m)T₁ = (n³/m³)L₁."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2019 · 10 Jan · Shift 1","tp":"units","st":"Changing units","q":"The density of a material in SI units is 128 kg m⁻³. In certain units in which the unit of length is 25 cm and the unit of mass is 50 g, the numerical value of the density of the material is","o":["640","16","40","410"],"c":2,"s":"The new unit of density = 50 g/(25 cm)³ = 0.05 kg/(0.25 m)³ = 3.2 kg m⁻³. A bigger unit gives a smaller number: 128/3.2 = 40."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2023 · 11 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"units","st":"Light-year, parsec and AU","q":"Statement I: Astronomical unit (AU), parsec (pc) and light year (ly) are units for measuring astronomical distances.\nStatement II: AU < parsec (pc) < ly.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer.","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"c":1,"s":"All three are units of distance, so Statement I is correct. 1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m, 1 ly = 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m and 1 pc = 3.08 × 10¹⁶ m. So AU < ly < pc, and Statement II is wrong."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2022 · 27 Jun · Shift 2","tp":"units","st":"Parallax and angular size","q":"The distance of the Sun from the Earth is 1.5 × 10¹¹ m and its angular diameter is 2000 s when observed from the Earth. The diameter of the Sun will be","o":["2.45 × 10¹⁰ m","1.45 × 10¹⁰ m","1.45 × 10⁹ m","0.14 × 10⁹ m"],"c":2,"s":"2000″ = 2000/3600 degree = 9.7 × 10⁻³ rad. Diameter = distance × angle = 1.5 × 10¹¹ × 9.7 × 10⁻³ = 1.45 × 10⁹ m."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2021 · 27 Aug · Shift 1","tp":"units","st":"Parallax and angular size","q":"A huge circular arc of length 4.4 ly subtends an angle of 4″ at the centre of the circle. How long will it take for a body to complete 4 revolutions if its speed is 8 AU per second? Given 1 ly = 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m, 1 AU = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m","o":["3.5 × 10⁶ s","4.5 × 10¹⁰ s","4.1 × 10⁸ s","7.2 × 10⁸ s"],"c":1,"s":"4″ = 1.94 × 10⁻⁵ rad. Radius = 4.4 × 9.46 × 10¹⁵ / 1.94 × 10⁻⁵ = 2.15 × 10²¹ m. Distance for 4 revolutions = 4(2πR) = 5.4 × 10²² m. Speed = 8 × 1.5 × 10¹¹ = 1.2 × 10¹² m/s, so t = 4.5 × 10¹⁰ s."},
{"y":"JEE Main 2024 · 8 Apr · Shift 1","tp":"units","st":"Order of magnitude","q":"In an expression a × 10^b,","o":["b is the order of magnitude for 5 < a ≤ 10","b is the order of magnitude for a ≤ 5","b is the order of magnitude for a ≥ 5","a is the order of magnitude for b ≤ 5"],"c":1,"s":"The order of magnitude is b when a is up to 5. When a is more than 5 it is rounded up to 10, so the order is b + 1."}
];
